/**
 * @page xpost_roots The root set
 *
 * What the garbage collector begins its mark phase from, and which bank of
 * virtual memory each thing reachable from it lives in. @ref design_gc is
 * the collector itself; this page is its root set, thing by thing.
 *
 * @section roots_banks Two banks
 *
 * An execution context has two memory files: local and global. Which one an
 * allocation lands in is chosen by the allocation mode, which @c setglobal
 * alters and @c currentglobal reports.
 *
 * PLRM 3.7.2 gives the rule that shapes everything below: a global object may
 * not reference a local one. Local may reference global freely. So a local
 * dictionary holding a global array is ordinary, and the reverse raises
 * @c invalidaccess. Six dictionaries @c systemdict holds are the sanctioned
 * exception, and the collector marks them as such.
 *
 * PLRM 3.7.3 exempts global virtual memory from @c save and @c restore: what a
 * program puts there survives a restore that ends the save it was made in.
 * PLRM 3.7.4 states the consequence for reclamation -- garbage collection is
 * the only way storage in global virtual memory is ever recovered, since save
 * and restore do not reach it.
 *
 * Both banks are collected. A collection marks them together in one pass,
 * because an object in one may be named from the other and a walk that
 * stopped at the boundary would take a named object for garbage, and then
 * reclaims whichever banks it was asked for. A bank is reclaimed only by a
 * collection that marked it, which is what PLRM 8.2's @c vmreclaim chooses
 * between: it turns automatic collection off for one bank or for both, and
 * performs an immediate collection of one or of both.
 *
 * Everything below is therefore a root in earnest -- an object reachable
 * only through one of them, and not marked, is taken by the next collection
 * that runs.
 *
 * @section roots_tree The tree
 *
 * In the tree below, (G) is global and (L) is local.
 *
 * @verbatim
 * COLLECTOR ROOTS
 * |
 * +- special entities -- below `start`, never swept ----------- per bank
 * |    FREE . SAVE_STACK . CONTEXT_LIST . NAME_STACK . NAME_TREE
 * |    BOGUS_NAME
 * |
 * +- save stack ......................... both banks
 * |    +- the backups a save took        (G) and (L), one stack each
 * |         the descent into a backup's members crosses banks with the
 * |         rest of the walk, so a backed-up composite marks what it
 * |         holds in the other bank
 * |
 * +- name stack ......................... both banks
 * |    +- (L) interned names
 * |    +- (G) interned names
 * |         a name is interned into the bank the allocation mode
 * |         selects, so both stacks hold names; each name's characters
 * |         are an entity of their own, above `start`
 * |
 * +- the operator table's procedures ...  (G)
 * |         the table is the memory file's own storage and no part of
 * |         either arena, so the walk does not reach it; what keeps the
 * |         procedures alive is .wrappedprocs, below
 * |
 * +- per context
 *    +- os    operand stack ............. (L)
 *    +- es    execution stack ........... (L)
 *    +- hold ............................ (L)  an operator's temporaries
 *    +- ds    dictionary stack .......... (L)
 *    |   +- systemdict ................................. (G)
 *    |   |   +- the language: operators and procedures .. (G)
 *    |   |   +- sanctioned exceptions (PLRM 3.7.2) ...... (L)
 *    |   |       userdict . $error . errordict . statusdict
 *    |   |       serverdict . FontDirectory
 *    |   +- userdict ................................... (L)
 *    |
 *    +- the objects the context holds on its own account, declared and
 *    |  walked from one list (XPOST_CONTEXT_OBJECT_ROOTS):
 *    |   +- currentobject ............... either
 *    |   +- executingarray .............. either  the array a procedure
 *    |   |                                        is being run out of
 *    |   +- arcstartproc ................ (G)  built while the arc
 *    |   |                                     operators are installed
 *    |   +- pagedevice .................. (L)  device instance dictionary
 *    |   |   +- ImgData ................. (G)  array of
 *    |   |        +- row strings ........ (G)  the raster
 *    |   +- pagedevice_destroy .......... (L)
 *    |   +- privatedict ................. (L)  local machinery
 *    |   |   +- device classes .......... (L)
 *    |   |   +- .gscratch / .gtemplate .. (L)
 *    |   |   +- .error .................. (L)
 *    |   |   +- .wrappedprocs ........... (L) holding (G) procedures
 *    |   +- globalprivatedict = .xpostsys  (G)  global machinery
 *    |   |   +- helper procedures ....... (G)
 *    |   |   +- .resources .............. (G)  resource instance table
 *    |   |   +- .facecache .............. (G)  one entry per face
 *    |   |        +- /<face name> ....... (G)  /CharStrings, /sfnts
 *    |   +- run_input_file .............. (L)
 *    |   +- window_device ............... (L)
 *    |   +- event_handler ............... either
 *    |   +- localfontdir / globalfontdir  (L) / (G)
 * @endverbatim
 *
 * @section roots_members Notes on particular members
 *
 * <b>The raster.</b> @c ImgData and its row strings are global, so that a mark
 * made inside a @c save is still on the page after the restore that ends it,
 * which is what PLRM 3.7.3 requires of raster memory. They hang from the
 * device instance dictionary, which is a root, so they are reachable while
 * the device is installed and become garbage when @c setpagedevice replaces it
 * or the job ends.
 *
 * <b>The two private namespaces.</b> @c .xpostsys is global and @c privatedict is
 * local, and the split is forced rather than stylistic: @c privatedict holds
 * local objects -- the device classes, the graphics scratch -- which a global
 * dictionary may not hold. @c .xpostsys is recorded in the context, which
 * roots it directly and gives C a route to it. @c .internaldict is global and
 * is reached through the references frozen into procedure bodies, and through
 * the @c internaldict operator, whose own procedure holds one such reference.
 * Both namespaces drop their @c userdict anchor when they are sealed.
 * @ref xpost_dicts is the whole of that layout.
 *
 * <b>Names.</b> @c addname interns into the bank the allocation mode selects, so
 * a name first seen while allocation was global lives in global virtual
 * memory and one first seen while it was local lives in local. Both name
 * stacks are therefore populated in an ordinary run. A name object carries
 * the index of its characters on the stack of its own bank, and the stack
 * itself is a special entity, below @c start, so a sweep never takes it.
 *
 * <b>The operator table.</b> An operator that runs a procedure holds that
 * procedure in the table, and the table is not in virtual memory at all:
 * it is the memory file's own storage, because a signature carries the C
 * function implementing the operator and the arena holds no addresses. So
 * the collector does not reach it, and the procedures are kept alive by
 * @c .wrappedprocs, which it does reach.
 *
 * The same split is why the job boundary keeps a copy of the table. The
 * boundary reverts both banks to a baseline image, which cannot reach
 * storage outside them -- and a row holds objects of the global bank, so
 * a job that wrapped an operator would otherwise leave a row naming an
 * entity the revert had dropped, which the next collection follows.
 *
 * <b>The font face cache.</b> A face is cached so that every font dictionary a
 * name produces shares one synthesized @c CharStrings dictionary and one
 * @c sfnts array. The cache is split: a C structure keeps the face the font
 * library opened and the file it came from, and the objects live in
 * @c .xpostsys @c /.facecache, one dictionary per face. The dictionary is made
 * while the boot files load, because the namespace is sealed read-only
 * afterwards and a member added later would be refused; the seal is
 * shallow, so writing into it goes on working.
 *
 * @section roots_outside Reachability outside virtual memory
 *
 * An object held in a variable or a structure of C is reached by nothing
 * the collector walks, so the first collection takes it and the holder goes
 * on using whatever the storage has become. Such a holder must name its
 * object somewhere the walk does reach. There are three ways, and
 * @c tests/c_held_objects.register records which each holder uses: the
 * context holds it and the root list above covers it; an entity the walk
 * covers names it, as a file entity names the procedure a procedure-source
 * stream reads from; or it is reachable from an object already covered.
 *
 * The register is checked by @c tests/check-c-held-objects.sh, which fails
 * on a holder that is not in it. The collector cannot be taught to look in
 * a variable it has never heard of, so nothing else would catch one.
 *
 * The converse case is a holder with nothing to register. A device's
 * C-level instance state is a block outside virtual memory, reached through
 * the handle its instance dictionary carries under @c /Private, so it is
 * exempt from @c restore the way raster memory is (PLRM 3.7.3). A recording
 * device's marks are held there: the record holds no @c Xpost_Object at all,
 * so there is nothing for the walk to reach and nothing to register, and
 * past a threshold its bytes are in an unnamed scratch file and are not in
 * the process's memory either. So @c restore does not reach a record and
 * @c vmstatus does not count it, however large the drawing it holds. What
 * the walk does reach is the device instance dictionary, and the record
 * goes when that device is retired and its @c Destroy runs.
 *
 * The rasters of the compiled devices that keep a buffer of their own --
 * png, jpeg, raster and BGR -- are outside virtual memory for the same
 * reason and are likewise uncounted; the raster classes' @c ImgData rows,
 * above, are the ones @c vmstatus sees.
 *
 * @section roots_keys Keys that outlive a restore
 *
 * The section above asks whether the collector can still find a thing. This
 * one asks whether a key still names what it named. They are different
 * questions: naming an object to the collector keeps it from being swept
 * and says nothing about @c restore, which winds local virtual memory back
 * whether or not the walk could reach it.
 *
 * Several things here are held against a number that names them. The rule
 * those arrangements keep is:
 *
 * @par
 * No part of a key may live in storage more durable than the counter that
 * mints it.
 *
 * Three places matter, and they are easy to conflate because two of them
 * are usually the same:
 *
 * @li <b>the mint</b> -- where the number comes from.
 * @li <b>the stamp</b> -- where it is written so the thing can be recognised
 *     again. This is often the program's own dictionary -- a font
 *     dictionary, a form dictionary -- and the bank of that dictionary is
 *     the program's choice, not this code's.
 * @li <b>the store</b> -- where whatever is filed under the number lives.
 *
 * Durability runs in three steps. A value in local virtual memory is wound
 * back by @c restore. A value in global virtual memory is not (PLRM 3.7.3).
 * A value in a C variable, or bytes already written to a file, is not
 * reached by any winding back at all.
 *
 * Break the rule and the counter goes back below a number that is still
 * being carried somewhere more durable. The next thing minted is issued
 * that number, two things are filed under one key, and whichever arrived
 * first answers for both. Nothing raises. A glyph is drawn from another
 * font's mask, a form paints another form's marks, a fill is cut by another
 * clip's shape. It is only ever visible as the wrong thing on a page, which
 * is why each of these is driven by a test that counts rather than one that
 * asks whether an error was raised: a cache that is never consulted raises
 * none either.
 *
 * Two arrangements satisfy the rule, and a third sidesteps it:
 *
 * @li <b>Mint outside virtual memory.</b> The counter only moves forward, so no
 *     number is issued twice and the stamp and the store may then live
 *     anywhere. This is what the glyph masks, the form drawings and the
 *     resolved regions do.
 * @li <b>Mint, stamp and store all in one bank</b>, so a restore takes all three
 *     back together and a reissued number finds nothing filed under it.
 *     Sound, but only while all three stay there -- and the stamp is the
 *     one that tends to escape, because it is written into a dictionary
 *     the program supplied.
 * @li <b>Mint nothing.</b> A key derived from the content, checked against a
 *     stored copy of that content, cannot be reissued because it was never
 *     issued.
 *
 * What is held, and where each part lives:
 *
 * <table>
 * <tr><th>Held<th>Mint<th>Stamp<th>Store</tr>
 * <tr><td>glyph masks
 *     <td>@c .newfontserial (C)
 *     <td>@c .fontid in the font dictionary, either bank
 *     <td>C, outside virtual memory. The selector half of the key is a
 *         name's stack index together with the bank bit, since a name is
 *         that pair and nothing narrower tells two of them apart.
 *         (@c tests/font_serial_test.ps)</tr>
 * <tr><td>form drawings
 *     <td>@c .newformserial (C)
 *     <td>@c .formid in the form dictionary, either bank
 *     <td>@c .formcache in @c graphicsdict, local. The store is wound back by
 *         a restore, which costs a re-capture and never a wrong drawing,
 *         because the serial an entry was filed under is not issued again.
 *         (@c tests/form_serial_restore_test.ps)</tr>
 * <tr><td>resolved regions,<br>read-back clip bands
 *     <td>@c .newregionserial (C)
 *     <td>@c serial in the clip cache dictionary, local
 *     <td>C. The entity the clip array occupied is compared as well, so a
 *         restarted counter cannot match an entry built from something
 *         else.</tr>
 * <tr><td>@c clipPath identities
 *     <td>a counter in global virtual memory, one per device
 *     <td>none
 *     <td>the document, whose written bytes cannot be taken back.
 *         (@c tests/run-svg-clipid-test.sh)</tr>
 * <tr><td>faces
 *     <td colspan="3">no mint: the key is the face name. The host half is in C
 *         and the object half is in a dictionary under the global private
 *         namespace, so both halves are equally durable.</tr>
 * <tr><td>stencil runs
 *     <td colspan="3">no mint: the key is a content hash, and a match is
 *         confirmed against a stored copy of the mask, so a collision
 *         answers no differently than a miss.</tr>
 * </table>
 *
 * A counter that runs its range restarts below numbers it has already
 * handed out, at which point what is held can no longer be told apart from
 * what the reissued numbers will name, and it has to be given up.
 * @c .newfontserial clears the mask cache and @c .newregionserial flushes the
 * region memo, both of which they can reach. @c .newformserial cannot reach
 * its cache, which is in virtual memory, so it restarts and the caller
 * notices the number is no longer greater than the last one it saw and
 * drops the cache itself; the number last seen is kept beside the cache so
 * that a restore takes both back together and the test stays true to what
 * the cache actually holds.
 *
 * @section roots_sweep Which bank a collection may sweep
 *
 * A bank may only be swept by a collection that marked it. The roots above
 * reach an object in the other bank only when the collection is asked for
 * both, so sweeping a bank this walk did not cover would take storage that
 * is still named.
 *
 * A collection that runs of its own accord is asked for both, because
 * marking has to cross them whatever is reclaimed and the sweep of the
 * second is a walk of its entity table and nothing more. @c vmreclaim
 * (PLRM 8.2) is the program's say in it: the negative operands turn the
 * automatic collection off for one bank or for both and zero turns it on
 * again, while 1 and 2 perform an immediate collection of local or of
 * both.
 */
